\caption{Figure: Cascaded, end-to-end, and interleaved speech systems. The tree contrasts three design regimes for spoken dialogue: cascaded ASR→LLM→TTS pipelines emphasizing control but risking error compounding and paralinguistic loss; unified end-to-end SLMs that reduce hand-offs, enable duplex interaction, and rely on thin projector bridges and targeted alignment (semantic, temporal, affect) to stabilize behavior; and interleaved/parallel decoding that structures when text and speech tokens appear to bound latency while preserving style via AR-on-semantics and NAR refinement. Evaluation guidance highlights multilingual SLU (IndicSUPERB), unsupervised ABX discriminability, and syntax probing to compare semantic vs paralinguistic competence across regimes.}
\label{fig:tiny_tree_figure_5}

